; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Spirograph
; CATEGORY: Generative Art & Colour
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     spirograph, hypotrochoid, SIN, COS, GCD, recursion
; SUMMARY:  Draws spirograph patterns from the formula for a wheel rolling inside a ring.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A spirograph toy has a small toothed wheel that rolls round
;   inside a big ring, with a pen in a hole of the wheel. This
;   program uses the math of that toy. Three different wheels draw
;   three looping patterns, laid on top of each other in different
;   colors.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change the three numbers in each SPIRO line: the
;   teeth on the ring, the teeth on the wheel, and how far from the
;   middle of the wheel the pen hole is.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * the hypotrochoid formula with SIN and COS
;   * the greatest common divisor, found by Euclid's recursion
;   * knowing when a pattern closes: after wheel / gcd trips round
;   * scaling any design to fit the screen
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Try 100 25 25. Why is it so simple?
;   * Try 100 99 80. The wheel barely fits; the pattern is huge.
;   * Make the pen hole bigger than the wheel (like 100 30 60).
; ============================================================

; GCD finds the greatest common divisor of two whole numbers. If b
; divides a exactly, b is the answer; otherwise ask again with b and
; the remainder. Euclid knew this over two thousand years ago.
to gcd :a :b
  if :b = 0 [output :a]
  output gcd :b remainder :a :b
end

; SPIRO draws one pattern. :ring and :wheel are teeth counts, :hole
; is how far the pen is from the wheel's middle.
to spiro :ring :wheel :hole :color
  localmake "k (:ring - :wheel) / :wheel
  ; Grow or shrink so the farthest point is 270 from the middle.
  localmake "scale 270 / (:ring - :wheel + :hole)
  ; The wheel must go round the ring this many times to come home.
  localmake "laps :wheel / gcd :ring :wheel
  setpencolor :color
  penup
  for [t 0 360 * :laps 2] [
    ; The brackets around (cos :t) matter: without them COS would
    ; swallow everything after it, because + and * are worked out
    ; before COS gets its input.
    setxy :scale * ((:ring - :wheel) * (cos :t) + :hole * (cos :k * :t)) ~
          :scale * ((:ring - :wheel) * (sin :t) - :hole * (sin :k * :t))
    pendown
  ]
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [250 248 240]
setpensize 1.2
spiro 96 60 50 [220 60 90]
spiro 105 40 30 [40 110 200]
spiro 120 45 80 [40 160 110]
